# Why this file exists — measured on this repo, not inherited.
#
# setuptools' legacy sdist default matches only `tests/test*.py`. On this repo
# that is a much larger hole than the name suggests, because the suite is mostly
# nested: the pattern matches nothing below `tests/`, so the sdist shipped
# **14 of 50 test modules** — every module under tests/core/, tests/data/,
# tests/integrations/, tests/intelligence/, tests/optimizer/, tests/renderers/,
# tests/sections/, tests/tables/, tests/validation/ and tests/visualization/ was
# silently dropped, along with tests/conftest.py and tests/__init__.py. 72% of
# the suite was missing from the tarball.
#
# The 14 that did ship could not run either. Installing the published 1.1.5
# sdist and running its own tests/ interrupts at collection with 2 errors and
# executes ZERO tests, because four modules import from `streamlit_app/`
# (resolved as `os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(__file__))`) which the tarball
# did not carry. Ignoring those four leaves 39 passed / 6 failed / 12 errors,
# the failures and errors all being tests that read `templates/` — also absent.
#
# So this file has to ship three things, not one:
#   tests/        the whole suite, nested packages included
#   templates/    read by test_sample_yaml_score, test_template_fields,
#                 test_template_roundtrip and test_csv_robustness
#   streamlit_app/  imported by the four test_streamlit_* modules
#
# A note on the conftest fixtures, because it is easy to get backwards: the nine
# fixtures in tests/conftest.py are referenced by ZERO of the 14 top-level test
# modules and by 30 of the 36 nested ones. They are not dead code — they are
# used exclusively by the part of the suite that was never shipped. (Two of the
# nine, passing_validation and failing_validation, do have no references
# anywhere; that is a source question, not a packaging one.)
#
# This reproduces ONLY from a clean checkout. A local `python -m build` in a
# working tree that already has `nmtc_application_builder.egg-info/` reuses the
# stale SOURCES.txt and produces a CORRECT sdist. Since `*.egg-info/` is
# gitignored, CI never has that seed — so the artifact CI ships is broken while
# the one a maintainer builds by hand looks fine. Verified: the file list of the
# 1.1.5 sdist on PyPI is identical to a fresh-clone build.
#
# EIGHTH instance of this defect in the portfolio: sbic-tracker, oz-tracker,
# cdfi-fund-tracker and nmtc-mapper hit it first, then cdfi-benchmark,
# hmda-analyzer and cdfi-data as five, six and seven. This repo is the last of
# a four-repo sweep, and the only one where the build-side test-module count
# assertion fires at all -- see the note on that step in release.yml. (Do not
# copy this ordinal forward unchanged -- four files all claiming to be the
# fifth is how the count stops being evidence.) The fix was written four times
# and propagated nowhere, so this change is paired with a `test-sdist` job
# in release.yml that unpacks the built tarball and runs ITS suite. The
# MANIFEST.in is only what makes that job pass; the job is what stops this from
# silently regressing. Packaging that is never installed from is packaging that
# is never verified.
recursive-include tests *.py

# tests/invariant_allowlist.txt — the reviewed allowlist for the invariance
# gate. NOT a .py file, so `recursive-include tests *.py` missed it, and the
# sdist shipped test_invariant_output.py with no allowlist to read: 3 failed
# inside the tarball while the same suite was green in the repo. Caught by the
# test-sdist job, which is the whole reason that job exists. The gate fails
# closed on a missing allowlist rather than passing vacuously, which is why
# this showed up as a red build and not as silence.
recursive-include tests *.txt

# tests/cli_baseline_replay.json — the twenty recorded nmtc-mapper answers the
# CLI baseline replays. THIRD instance of the same hole, and it was caught
# before shipping this time only because 1.4.0 built the tarball by hand and
# listed it: `recursive-include tests *.py` misses it, `*.txt` misses it, and
# tests/test_cli_baseline.py inside the sdist would have raised
# FileNotFoundError on a fixture the repo has. Identical in shape to the
# invariant_allowlist.txt line above, which shipped red and was found by CI.
#
# The pattern is deliberately by extension rather than by name: a second data
# fixture in any other format is the fourth instance waiting to happen, so
# widen this line when one arrives rather than adding a third include.
recursive-include tests *.json

include README.md
include CHANGELOG.md
include CONTRIBUTING.md
include CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
include CITATION.cff
include pyproject.toml

# Data files the shipped suite reads. Not documentation — remove these and
# test_template_fields, test_template_roundtrip, test_csv_robustness and
# test_sample_yaml_score all fail inside the tarball.
#
# 1.2.0 moved these from the repo root into nmtcapp/templates/ and declared
# them as [tool.setuptools.package-data]. That is what puts them in the
# WHEEL, which this file never governed — the sdist was always fine, so the
# `nmtcapp init` failure reproduced only for pip users. This line is kept so
# the sdist carries them explicitly rather than relying on package-data.
recursive-include nmtcapp/templates *.yaml *.csv *.xlsx

# Imported by the four test_streamlit_* modules, which put the repo root and
# streamlit_app/ on sys.path and then `import chart_style` / `import utils`.
# Without this the sdist suite does not merely fail, it ABORTS at collection.
recursive-include streamlit_app *.py *.txt *.md

# Repo artifacts, not part of the distributed source: the built docs site, the
# mkdocs config that generates it, one-off scripts, and the example notebooks.
prune site
prune docs
prune examples
prune scripts

global-exclude *.pyc
global-exclude __pycache__/*
